      "created_at": "2018-10-27T16:12:31Z",
      "updated_at": "2018-10-27T16:23:12Z",
      "body": "This is the special agreement: https://developers.cloudflare.com/1.1.1.1/commitment-to-privacy/privacy-policy/firefox/\r\n\r\nThe privacy policy for 1.1.1.1 is instead: https://developers.cloudflare.com/1.1.1.1/commitment-to-privacy/privacy-policy/privacy-policy/\r\n\r\nIn both cases you can see:\r\n> All information collected by Cloudflare, no matter whether such information is part of Cloudflare’s temporary or permanent logs, will be cleansed of any personally identifiable data (including your IP addresses). Additionally information that is stored as part of Cloudflare’s permanent logs will be further anonymized.\r\n\r\nAn important question: is the main difference between the two that Cloudflare is sharing this DNS data with Mozilla? It is not stated explicitly that it is not the case.\r\n\r\nThe only difference I can see is that the data used for Cloudflare permanent logs contains less fields, but I would like to know if there is an agreement between Cloudflare and Mozilla about sharing such data first.\r\n\r\n**Edit:** I think the wording gives away the relationship between Cloudflare and Mozilla:\r\n> Any data Cloudflare handles as a result of its resolver for Firefox is as a date [sic] processor acting pursuant to Firefox’s data processing instructions.\r\n\r\n(date is a typo, should be data)\r\n\r\nThis means Firefox (they mean Mozilla?) is the data controller second GDPR parlance, thus it also has access to the data.\r\n\r\nIf this is the case, then I deny this request: we do not need to share the data with two companies instead of one.",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "pin": null,
